---
name: polish-review
description: >-
  Review a live URL, a screenshot, or pasted UI code for the details that make
  an interface look cheap or premium (alignment, spacing, radius, type, colour,
  states, mobile). Returns a ranked P0/P1/P2 punch list with exact fixes. Use
  when the user sends a link or screenshot and asks "what's wrong with this",
  "make it look better", "is this ready to launch", or "why does this look
  AI-made".
---
# Polish review

The job: find what makes this screen look unfinished, prove it with a measurement or a crop, and give a fix specific enough to paste into a coding agent. Taste words ("cleaner", "more modern") are not findings. Numbers are.

## 1. Intake

Work with whatever arrived. Don't ask for a better input before showing a first result.

| Input | What to do |
| --- | --- |
| Live URL | Open it in your browser. This is the best case: measure, don't guess. |
| Screenshot | Judge what's visible. Say once if it is too small or compressed to measure, and give approximate values marked "approx". |
| Screen recording | Hand motion questions to the `motion-review` skill. Review stills from it here. |
| Pasted code or a repo link | Read it, then review the rendered result if there's a URL. Cite file and line. |
| Figma link | Ask for a screenshot or an exported frame unless Figma is connected. |

If the page needs a login, ask the user to send screenshots of the signed-in screens instead. Never ask for their password.

## 2. Look before measuring (URL)

1. Load the page at 1440px wide. Screenshot the first screen and a full-page capture. Long pages make very tall captures (8,000px+); cut them into viewport-height slices before looking, or details get lost.
2. Load it again at 390px (a phone). Scroll the whole page. Screenshot anything that breaks. Read the headline and lead closely at this width: awkward breaks (a hyphenated word split across lines, one word alone on a line) show up here.
3. If the site has a theme toggle or follows the system theme, check the other theme too. If it only has one theme, say so and skip this.
4. Click or tab through the main controls: buttons, inputs, menus, tabs, dialogs. Menus are often `<details>/<summary>` or buttons with `aria-expanded`, not just `<button>`. On a phone, open the nav menu and check the trigger shows its open state. Note any control with no visible hover, press, focus, disabled, loading, empty, or error state.
5. Tab through the page once with the keyboard (about 15 stops is enough on a landing page). Every focusable thing needs a visible focus ring. Read `outline` and `box-shadow` on `document.activeElement` at each stop if the ring is hard to see in a screenshot.

## 3. Measure (URL)

Run the measurement script (at the end of this skill) in the page at 390px and at 1440px. It returns JSON with:

- `overflow`: whether the page scrolls sideways and, only if it does, which elements stick out. Sideways scroll on a phone is always a P0.
- `type`: every font size, weight and family in use; text under 12px, split into short uppercase labels (badges like NEW, PRO, initials, which are an accepted exception at 11px) and everything else; numbers missing `tabular-nums`.
- `radius`: every radius in use, and nested corners that are not concentric.
- `controls`: control heights in use, targets under 24px (with their size), icon buttons with no accessible name.
- `colour`: text under 4.5:1 contrast (3:1 for large text) against its nearest solid background (oklch, lab and other modern colour formats are converted to RGB first), distinct text colours, and shadows.
- `motion`: how many elements use `transition: all` and which, the transitions in use, running animations with their play state, and how many `prefers-reduced-motion` rules exist.
- `content`: images with no alt, and placeholder text (lorem ipsum, Acme, $0.00).

Screen-reader-only content (for example the hidden data table behind a chart) is skipped automatically.

Treat the output as leads. Confirm each one on screen before reporting it. Known non-findings:
- `numbersWithoutTabular` only matters for numbers that update (counters, prices that change, timers) or line up in a column. A version number, an order ID or a percentage in a sentence is fine.
- `smallTargets`: inline text links inside a sentence are exempt from target size. Controls whose `::before`/`::after` extends the hit area are already filtered out.
- `lowContrast` can't judge text on gradients or images. Check those by eye and say "approx".
- Several font families can be fine (a display face, a mono for code). A family used on only a handful of elements, or in one paragraph, usually isn't.

The script flags targets under 24px. On a phone, also check the primary controls (menu button, main CTA) against 44px by hand.

## 4. Check against the rules

These are the checks that separate a premium interface from a generic one. Each has a number so a finding can be proven.

**Alignment and grouping**
- One left edge per column. Count distinct left edges inside a card; more than two in a single column is a finding.
- Gap inside a group is at most half the gap between groups. Typical: label to input 4–8px, field to field 16–24px, section to section 32–48px, page sections 64–96px.
- Spacing comes from a 4px scale. Odd values (13px, 18px, 22px) usually mean hand-tuned drift.
- Everything in one row shares one height.

**Corners, borders, depth**
- Nested corners are concentric: inner radius = outer radius minus the padding between them. A 16px card with 8px padding holds an 8px inner element, not another 16px one.
- Radius scales with size: 24px chip about 6px, 32px button about 8px, 40px button about 10px, card 16px, dialog or sheet about 24px. Full pills only on small controls or one hero CTA.
- One way to separate a surface: a border or a shadow, rarely both at full strength. Shadows are soft, layered, low alpha (light mode at or under about 0.1), never pure black glows.
- At most three surface levels visible at once.

**Colour**
- Neutral first. One accent, used once per region for the primary action or the one number that matters.
- Body text contrast at least 4.5:1, UI and large text at least 3:1.
- Dark mode is its own palette: a near-black canvas (not #000), raised surfaces slightly lighter, stronger shadows, not an inverted light theme.

**Type**
- Two sizes per card, three per block. Weights 400, 500, 600. Nothing under 12px that someone needs to read.
- Weight never changes on hover or selection (it shifts the layout).
- Numbers that update or sit in columns use `tabular-nums`.
- Tracking tightens as size grows: body near 0, 24px about -0.02em, display about -0.03em.
- Headings use `text-wrap: balance`; short paragraphs use `pretty`.

**Controls and states**
- Heights from a small set (24, 32, 36, 40, 48px). Horizontal padding about 0.4 to 0.5 times the height.
- Hover only on devices with a fine pointer, changes colour by one step, never size or position.
- Visible focus ring on every interactive element (2px ring, 2px offset is a safe default). `outline: none` with no replacement is a P0.
- Every state designed: hover, pressed, focus, disabled, loading, empty, error, success.
- Targets at least 24px, 44px for isolated touch targets.
- Destructive actions confirm or offer undo.

**Content**
- Real content: real names, realistic numbers that go up and down, real timestamps. Lorem ipsum, "John Doe", and every metric trending up all read as a template.
- One focal point per block. Four equal cards in a row has no hierarchy.

**Responsive**
- No sideways scroll at 360px or 390px.
- Nothing important truncated ("Low T…"). Nav and the primary CTA stay reachable on a phone.
- Decorative graphics never sit on top of body text on a phone.

**Consistency**
- The same component looks the same everywhere: one button height per context, one radius per size, one shadow per elevation.

For motion (durations, easing, exits, press feedback), run the `motion-review` skill on the two or three most important interactions and fold its findings in.

## 5. Severity

- **P0**: broken or embarrassing. Sideways scroll on a phone, no focus state, unreadable text, a control that does nothing, layout overlap, placeholder content on a live page.
- **P1**: visibly below the bar, or a real bug. Non-concentric corners on the main card, three different button heights, an accent used everywhere, missing states, no reduced-motion handling.
- **P2**: polish. Odd spacing values, tracking, a slightly heavy shadow.

Rank by what it costs the person using the screen, then by how many places it appears.

## 6. Output

Lead with the verdict in one sentence. Then:

1. **Top 5, in plain English.** Written for someone who doesn't write CSS. One line each, what they'll notice and why it matters.
2. **Punch list table.**

   | # | Sev | Where | What's wrong (measured) | Fix (exact) |
   | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   | 1 | P0 | Pricing grid, 390px | Page is 498px wide, scrolls sideways | Add `min-w-0` to each grid child; let the plan cards stack below 640px |

3. **Systemic issues.** Patterns behind several findings (for example "six different radii for three sizes").
4. **Consistency contract.** 5 to 8 lines the user can keep: button heights, radius per size, spacing steps, one accent, focus ring.
5. **Offer the next step**: "Want a fix prompt for your coding agent?" (the `fix-prompt` skill), or a motion check.

Include screenshots or crops for the top findings when you have them. Cap the table at about 15 rows on first pass and say how many more exist.

## Rules for yourself

- Never invent a hex value, size, or duration. If you didn't measure it, say "approx" or leave it out.
- Praise what's already good in one line. People act on reviews that are fair.
- Don't redesign the page or change its brand. Fix what's there.
- Don't log in, submit forms, buy anything, or click destructive actions on someone's site while reviewing it.

## Measurement script

Save this to a file and paste it into the page (DevTools console or your browser tool's "run JS"). It returns a JSON summary.

```js
(() => {
  const vw = document.documentElement.clientWidth;
  const hiddenCache = new Map();
  const srOnly = el => {
    if (!el || el === document.body) return false;
    if (hiddenCache.has(el)) return hiddenCache.get(el);
    const cs = getComputedStyle(el), r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
    const hidden = cs.clipPath === 'inset(50%)' || /rect\(0(px)?,? 0(px)?,? 0(px)?,? 0(px)?\)/.test(cs.clip) ||
      (r.width <= 1 && r.height <= 1 && cs.overflow === 'hidden') || srOnly(el.parentElement);
    hiddenCache.set(el, hidden); return hidden;
  };
  const all = [...document.querySelectorAll('body *')].filter(el => {
    const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
    const cs = getComputedStyle(el);
    return r.width > 0 && r.height > 0 && cs.visibility !== 'hidden' && cs.display !== 'none' && !srOnly(el);
  });
  const label = el => {
    const id = el.id ? '#' + el.id : '';
    const cls = (el.className && typeof el.className === 'string') ? '.' + el.className.trim().split(/\s+/).slice(0, 3).map(c => c.slice(0, 30)).join('.') : '';
    const txt = (el.innerText || el.getAttribute('aria-label') || '').trim().replace(/\s+/g, ' ').slice(0, 40);
    return `${el.tagName.toLowerCase()}${id}${cls}${txt ? ' "' + txt + '"' : ''}`;
  };
  const tally = (arr) => Object.entries(arr.reduce((m, v) => (m[v] = (m[v] || 0) + 1, m), {})).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
  const px = v => parseFloat(v) || 0;

  const doc = document.scrollingElement || document.documentElement;
  const overflow = { viewport: vw, scrollWidth: doc.scrollWidth, sidewaysScroll: doc.scrollWidth > vw };
  const clipped = el => { for (let a = el.parentElement; a; a = a.parentElement) { if (/auto|scroll|hidden|clip/.test(getComputedStyle(a).overflowX)) return true; } return false; };
  overflow.widest = !overflow.sidewaysScroll ? [] : all.filter(el => el.getBoundingClientRect().right > vw + 1 && !clipped(el))
    .map(el => ({ el: label(el), right: Math.round(el.getBoundingClientRect().right) }))
    .sort((a, b) => b.right - a.right).slice(0, 8);

  const textEls = all.filter(el => el instanceof HTMLElement && [...el.childNodes].some(n => n.nodeType === 3 && n.textContent.trim()));
  const fontSizes = tally(textEls.map(el => getComputedStyle(el).fontSize));
  const fontWeights = tally(textEls.map(el => getComputedStyle(el).fontWeight));
  const fontFamilies = tally(textEls.map(el => getComputedStyle(el).fontFamily.split(',')[0].trim()));
  const tinyAll = textEls.filter(el => px(getComputedStyle(el).fontSize) < 12);
  const isCapsLabel = el => { const cs = getComputedStyle(el); return (cs.textTransform === 'uppercase' || el.innerText === el.innerText.toUpperCase()) && el.innerText.trim().length <= 4 || (cs.textTransform === 'uppercase' && px(cs.letterSpacing) > 0); };
  const tinyText = { count: tinyAll.length, capsLabels: tinyAll.filter(isCapsLabel).length,
    other: tinyAll.filter(el => !isCapsLabel(el)).slice(0, 12).map(el => `${label(el)} ${getComputedStyle(el).fontSize}`) };
  const numbersWithoutTabular = textEls.filter(el => el.textContent.trim().length < 24 && /\d[\d,.:%]{2,}/.test(el.textContent) && el.children.length === 0 &&
    !/tabular-nums/.test(getComputedStyle(el).fontVariantNumeric)).slice(0, 8).map(label);

  const radiusOf = el => px(getComputedStyle(el).borderTopLeftRadius);
  const radii = tally(all.map(radiusOf).filter(r => r > 0 && r < 9999).map(r => r + 'px'));
  const notConcentric = [];
  for (const el of all) {
    const rIn = radiusOf(el); if (!rIn || rIn > 999) continue;
    const p = el.parentElement; if (!p) continue;
    const rOut = radiusOf(p); if (!rOut || rOut > 999) continue;
    const a = el.getBoundingClientRect(), b = p.getBoundingClientRect();
    const gap = Math.max(a.left - b.left, a.top - b.top);
    if (Math.min(a.left - b.left, a.top - b.top) < 0 || gap >= rOut) continue;
    const ideal = Math.max(2, rOut - gap);
    if (Math.abs(rIn - ideal) > 3) notConcentric.push({ el: label(el), inner: rIn, outer: rOut, gap: Math.round(gap), ideal: Math.round(ideal) });
  }

  const controls = all.filter(el => el.matches('button, a[href], input, select, textarea, [role="button"], [role="tab"], [role="switch"]'));
  const controlHeights = tally(controls.filter(el => !el.matches('a') || getComputedStyle(el).display !== 'inline').map(el => Math.round(el.getBoundingClientRect().height) + 'px'));
  const smallTargets = controls.filter(el => { const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
    const extendedHitArea = ['::before', '::after'].some(pe => { const ps = getComputedStyle(el, pe);
      return ps.content !== 'none' && ps.position === 'absolute' &&
        (getComputedStyle(el).position === 'static' || parseFloat(ps.top) < 0 || parseFloat(ps.left) < 0 ||
         (parseFloat(ps.width) >= 24 && parseFloat(ps.height) >= 24)); });
    return !extendedHitArea && r.width > 2 && r.height > 2 && r.left >= 0 && (r.width < 24 || r.height < 24); })
    .map(el => { const r = el.getBoundingClientRect(); return `${label(el)} ${Math.round(r.width)}x${Math.round(r.height)}`; }).slice(0, 10);
  const iconButtonsNoName = controls.filter(el => el.matches('button, [role="button"]') && !el.innerText.trim() && !el.getAttribute('aria-label') && !el.getAttribute('title')).slice(0, 8).map(label);

  const textColours = tally(textEls.map(el => getComputedStyle(el).color)).slice(0, 12);
  const cx = document.createElement('canvas').getContext('2d', { willReadFrequently: true });
  const toRgb = c => { cx.clearRect(0, 0, 1, 1); cx.fillStyle = '#000'; cx.fillStyle = c; cx.fillRect(0, 0, 1, 1); const d = cx.getImageData(0, 0, 1, 1).data; return `rgb(${d[0]}, ${d[1]}, ${d[2]})`; };
  const lum = c => { const m = (toRgb(c).match(/[\d.]+/g) || [0, 0, 0]).map(Number); return 0.2126 * ch(m[0]) + 0.7152 * ch(m[1]) + 0.0722 * ch(m[2]); };
  const ch = v => { v /= 255; return v <= 0.03928 ? v / 12.92 : Math.pow((v + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4); };
  const solidBg = el => { for (let a = el; a; a = a.parentElement) { const cs = getComputedStyle(a); if (cs.backgroundImage !== 'none') return null;
    const m = cs.backgroundColor.match(/[\d.]+/g); if (m && (m.length < 4 || +m[3] > 0.95)) return cs.backgroundColor; } return 'rgb(255, 255, 255)'; };
  const lowContrast = [];
  for (const el of textEls) { const cs = getComputedStyle(el); if (/rgba\(.*, 0\)$/.test(cs.color)) continue; const bg = solidBg(el); if (!bg) continue;
    const a = lum(cs.color), b = lum(bg), ratio = (Math.max(a, b) + 0.05) / (Math.min(a, b) + 0.05);
    const large = px(cs.fontSize) >= 24 || (px(cs.fontSize) >= 18.66 && +cs.fontWeight >= 700);
    if (ratio < (large ? 3 : 4.5)) lowContrast.push(`${label(el)} ${ratio.toFixed(2)}:1 (${cs.color} on ${bg})`); }
  const shadows = tally(all.map(el => getComputedStyle(el).boxShadow).filter(s => s !== 'none')).slice(0, 10);

  const transitionAll = all.filter(el => { const cs = getComputedStyle(el); return /(^|,\s*)all\b/.test(cs.transitionProperty) && px(cs.transitionDuration) > 0; }).slice(0, 10).map(label);
  const transitions = tally(all.map(el => getComputedStyle(el)).filter(cs => px(cs.transitionDuration) > 0)
    .map(cs => `${cs.transitionProperty} | ${cs.transitionDuration} | ${cs.transitionTimingFunction.replace(/linear\([^)]*\)/g, 'linear(spring curve)')}`)).slice(0, 15);
  const running = document.getAnimations().slice(0, 15).map(a => {
    const t = a.effect && a.effect.getTiming ? a.effect.getTiming() : {};
    return { target: a.effect && a.effect.target ? label(a.effect.target) : '?', duration: t.duration, easing: t.easing, iterations: t.iterations === Infinity ? 'infinite' : t.iterations, state: a.playState };
  });
  let reducedMotionRules = 0, unreadableSheets = 0;
  for (const s of document.styleSheets) {
    try { for (const r of s.cssRules) if (r.media && /prefers-reduced-motion/.test(r.media.mediaText)) reducedMotionRules++; }
    catch { unreadableSheets++; }
  }

  const imagesNoAlt = [...document.images].filter(i => !i.hasAttribute('alt')).length;
  const placeholderText = [...new Set(document.body.innerText.match(/lorem ipsum|john doe|jane doe|\bacme\b|example\.com|\$0\.00|\bxxx+\b/gi) || [])];

  return JSON.stringify({
    url: location.href, overflow,
    type: { fontSizes, fontWeights, fontFamilies, tinyText, numbersWithoutTabular },
    radius: { radii, notConcentric: notConcentric.slice(0, 10) },
    controls: { controlHeights, smallTargets, iconButtonsNoName },
    colour: { lowContrast: lowContrast.slice(0, 12), textColours, distinctShadows: shadows.length, shadows },
    motion: { transitionAllCount: all.filter(el => { const cs = getComputedStyle(el); return /(^|,\s*)all\b/.test(cs.transitionProperty) && px(cs.transitionDuration) > 0; }).length, transitionAll, transitions, running, reducedMotionRules, unreadableSheets },
    content: { imagesNoAlt, placeholderText }
  });
})();
```

---
name: motion-review
description: >-
  Check how an interface moves (hover, press, menus, dialogs, toasts, page and
  list transitions) against concrete timing and easing values, then return a
  motion spec with exact numbers. Use when the user says an interface feels
  janky, slow, cheap, too bouncy, or "not like Linear/Apple", sends a screen
  recording, or asks how something should animate.
---
# Motion review

Good motion is mostly invisible: fast feedback, exits quicker than entrances, nothing bouncing on the way out, and calm for people who ask for reduced motion. This skill measures what a page actually does and compares it to values that are known to feel right.

## 1. Intake

- **Live URL**: best. Open it, then probe the interactions (step 2).
- **Screen recording**: step through it frame by frame. At 60fps one frame is about 16.7ms, so count frames from the first visible change to rest to estimate duration. Say the numbers are estimates.
- **Code**: read the transition and animation values directly (CSS `transition`, Tailwind `duration-*`/`ease-*`, Framer Motion / Motion `transition` props, spring configs).
- **Description only** ("my modal feels cheap"): ask for a URL or a 10-second recording, and meanwhile give the default spec from step 4.

## 2. Probe a live page

Run the probe script (at the end of this skill) in the page once. It adds `__probe(selector, action, ms)`, which records every CSS transition, CSS animation and Web Animation that starts in the next `ms` (default 1200): target, property, duration, delay, easing. Actions: `click`, `tap`, `focus`, `key:Escape`, `hover`, `press`, `watch`.

The reliable way is real input plus `watch`. Start the probe without awaiting it, do the real hover, click or mouse-down with your browser tool, then read the result:

```js
window.__r = __probe('a.cta', 'watch', 1500)   // then hover / click / press for real
await window.__r
```

Why: synthetic events don't trigger CSS `:hover` or `:active`, and some menus ignore synthetic clicks or toggle twice on them (`tap` sends pointerdown/up plus click, which can toggle twice). The shortcuts `__probe(sel, 'click' | 'focus' | 'key:Escape')` are fine for quick checks, but if a probe comes back empty while something visibly moved, redo it with `watch`.

Before pressing a link, block navigation so the page doesn't leave: `document.addEventListener('click', e => e.preventDefault(), true)`. Reload afterwards.

Probe the open and the close of each overlay separately, since exits are where most problems hide. If a transition seems to be missing from the probe, sample the element's computed `opacity` and `transform` every 50ms after the real click; that always shows what moved and for how long.

Interactions worth probing, in order: primary button hover and press, hover on cards and links, opening and closing a menu or popover (including `<details>` menus and the phone nav), opening and closing a dialog or sheet, a toast, tab or segmented switches, and anything that animates on page load.

Also:
- Emulate `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` (DevTools rendering panel or your browser tool's emulation) and repeat one probe. Large movement should be gone or replaced by a fade.
- Watch the page for 10 seconds without touching it. Anything looping longer than 5 seconds needs a pause, and loops should stop when off screen. `document.getAnimations()` shows each loop's `playState`; scroll it out of view and check it reads `paused`.

## 3. Check against these values

**Timing**
- Feedback (press, hover, toggle) starts at once and finishes within 200ms. Hover and colour changes about 150ms.
- Duration steps: 100, 150, 200, 300, 500ms. Popover enter about 150–200ms, dialog enter 200–300ms. Very little UI motion needs more than 500ms.
- Exit is at most 0.75 times the enter duration, and travels at most half the enter distance.

**Easing**
- Enter: strong ease-out, `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`.
- General state changes: `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`.
- Exit: ease-in, `cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0)`.
- Moving between two on-screen positions: `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)`.
- Default `ease` or `linear` on UI movement is a finding.

**Springs** (for things that track a gesture or change shape)
- Snappy (press release, toggles, small UI): stiffness 520, damping 38, settles in about 350ms.
- Smooth (layout and size changes): visual duration 0.4s, no bounce.
- Bouncy (stiffness 400, damping 26): only for a deliberate playful moment, never on exits, never on something used 50 times a day.

**Enter and exit shape**
- Overlays enter with fade + a small rise (4–8px, toward their trigger) + scale from about 0.97. Never scale from 0.
- Exits: fade + 3px + scale to about 0.98, ease-in, shorter.
- Toasts can travel further (about 12px).

**Press feedback**
- Scale on press: about 0.96 for icon buttons, 0.97 for text buttons, 0.985 for anything wider than about 220px. Something like 0.8 feels broken.
- Triggers that open a popover or menu show press with colour, not scale (scaling shifts the anchor).
- Hover effects only for fine pointers (`@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)`).

**Lists and text**
- Stagger about 35ms per list item, 40ms per word, 16ms per character, 80ms per line. The whole stagger stays under 400ms.
- Page-load choreography runs once per block, then motion is driven only by state.

**Performance and access**
- Animate only transform, opacity, clip-path, filter and colour. Animating width, height, top, left or box-shadow per frame is a finding.
- `transition: all` is a finding (it animates things nobody meant to animate).
- Blur stays at or under 8px and about 250ms.
- A reduced-motion branch exists. With it on, keep fades and colour, drop travel and scale.

## 4. Output

1. **Verdict** in one sentence ("Feedback is fast, but every exit is slower than its entrance, which makes closing things feel sticky.").
2. **What you measured**: a short table per interaction.

   | Interaction | Now | Should be | Fix |
   | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   | Dialog close | 300ms, ease-out, scale to 0.9 | 150ms, ease-in, scale to 0.98 | `transition: opacity 150ms cubic-bezier(.7,0,.84,0), transform 150ms cubic-bezier(.7,0,.84,0)` |

3. **Severity** on each row: P0 (broken: no reduced-motion handling on large movement, motion that blocks input, layout jank), P1 (feels wrong: slow feedback, slow or bouncy exits, scale-from-0, `transition: all`), P2 (tuning).
4. **Motion spec**: a short block the user can keep, in the format their stack uses (CSS variables, Tailwind config, or Motion `transition` objects). Example:

   ```css
   --ease-enter: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
   --ease-exit: cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0);
   --ease-standard: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
   --dur-fast: 150ms; --dur-base: 200ms; --dur-slow: 300ms;
   --press-scale: 0.97; --press-scale-icon: 0.96;
   ```

5. Offer the `fix-prompt` skill to turn it into a prompt for their coding agent.

## Rules for yourself

- Measured numbers are reported as measured. Frame-counted numbers are marked "about".
- Don't recommend more motion than the product needs. Removing an animation is often the fix.
- Taste disagreements (a brand that wants a bounce) are fine. Say what the trade-off is and move on.

## Probe script

Save this to a file and paste it into the page once.

```js
window.__probe = async (selector, action = 'click', windowMs = 1200) => {
  const el = document.querySelector(selector);
  if (!el) return JSON.stringify({ error: 'no element for ' + selector });
  const seen = [];
  const name = n => n ? (n.tagName.toLowerCase() + (n.id ? '#' + n.id : '') + (typeof n.className === 'string' && n.className ? '.' + n.className.trim().split(/\s+/).slice(0, 2).join('.') : '')) : '?';
